  "updated": "2026-09-22T10:00:16.066867+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA wave of low pressure riding along the east-west draped boundary \nnow analyzed off to our south will reinvigorate showers and storms \nduring the early overnight hours Monday night. From roughly \nI-70 on north, where temperatures will hold in the low 50s, \nlight stratiform rain will be the theme with a persistent \neasterly wind. South of I- 70, temperatures are a bit warmer and\nfumes of MUCAPE will support higher coverage and intensity of \nshowers and embedded thunderstorms driven by an inverted surface\ntrough from the aforementioned low. Precipitable water values \nhave dipped a little bit back toward 1.5\", and as has been the \ncase for the past couple days, HREF and REFS probability for \n1\"/hour rainfall rates is low. It'll be the longer duration 3 \nhour amounts and associated river rises to monitor. Latest hi \nres guidance pushes the rain off to our east in the latter half\nof the overnight hours.\n\nThat surface trough won't budge much into Tuesday, and we'll see \ncoverage of showers increase again through the day. An upper trough \nwill approach from the west and begin to slow down and close off \ninto a low. The broad nature of the forcing with diffluent mid-level \nflow and the inverted trough is giving Tuesday more of a scattered \nshower type look rather than a more coherent shield of rain. PWAT \nvalues come down a touch more and the column dries a bit in the mid \nand upper levels, but showers will still be productive with the bulk \nof the moisture in the warm cloud layer. Still not concerned about \nhourly rates as the 10 km neighborhood probability for 1\"/hour in \nany given hour is 20% or less. 24 hour mean totals from HREF through \n8am Wednesday range from a quarter to three quarters of an inch, and \neven the 90th percentile peaks around 1.25-1.5 inches south of \nPittsburgh. While the flash flooding threat overall may be \ndecreasing especially after tonight, overflow/areal flooding threats \nremain. Thus, the Flood Watch continues through 12Z Wednesday. \nCounties west of Pittsburgh were dropped as these areas are expected \nto avoid the higher totals.\n\nSupport for rain continues through Wednesday and into Thursday as \nthe upper low develops with highest coverage shifting west into \neastern Ohio in tandem with reorientation of the surface trough and \nPVA around the closing upper low. Guidance actually favors coverage \ndecreasing into the afternoon and evening hours which makes sense as \nwe become more centered beneath the upper low and lose the forcing \nlocally, all while surface high pressure over New England begins its \nattempt to nudge southwest. We won't lose the support for continued \nrain until likely Thursday when the upper low exits and the surface \nhigh better establishes and kicks the surface boundary out. Longer \nrange ensembles then point toward a dry weekend locally as a coastal \nlow develops off the Mid-Atlantic and shortwave ridging between that \nand a trough across the Dakotas becomes our dominant feature.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nBeneath the cold upper low, with persistent easterly flow, and \nbeneath thick cloud cover and showers, NBM advertises a 60-70% \nchance that our I-80 areas and down the PA & WV ridges can't get\nabove 60F for highs on Tuesday, and similar probabilities \nremain in the ridges on Wednesday. Elsewhere, the goal posts \nspread from low 60s on the cool end to mid 60s on the warm end, \nand given the conditions in place, it's likely a better bet to \nhedge cooler. Minimum temperatures will remain fairly close to \nnormal given abundant moisture, but daytime highs will run 10 \ndegrees below normal through Thursday. So, even optimistically, \nmid 60s is likely our ceiling through Thursday before more \nseasonable temperatures return for the weekend.",
